<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sumela Monastery</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Panagia Sumela</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sumela Monastery is a Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> monastery built into a cliff in the Pontic Mountains near Maçka, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trabzon</a> in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. Dedicated to the Virgin, it was by tradition founded in the 4th century and flourished under the emperors of Trebizond, who granted it lands and privileges. Its monks left in 1923, and it is now a museum where an annual liturgy has been held again since 2010.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sumela-monastery.jpg" alt="Sumela Monastery" width="320" height="126" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bjørn Christian Tørrissen · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sumela_From_Across_Valley.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
